WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:08.656
Let's understand CoCurve — a smarter way to prune large language models, using only a little calibration text and no fine-tuning.

2
00:00:08.836 --> 00:00:15.004
One shift in perspective: stop ranking parts one by one, and start preserving the connections between them.

3
00:00:15.424 --> 00:00:24.616
A language model is built from attention heads and feed-forward groups. Structured pruning deletes whole parts, so it runs lighter on real hardware.

4
00:00:25.036 --> 00:00:33.700
Which parts? Almost every method scores each part alone, then assumes a set's damage is the sum of those scores. That hides a wrong assumption.

5
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:43.600
Because damage isn't additive: remove one unit and a neighbor quietly covers for it; remove both and quality breaks — far more than the two scores predicted.

6
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:53.404
But a Transformer isn't a bag of independent parts — every layer reads and writes one shared residual stream, so parts interact when removed together.

7
00:00:53.824 --> 00:01:02.704
That creates bridge units: low-scoring alone, but propping up the parts that depend on them. Score in isolation, and you delete the bridge with what it held up.

8
00:01:03.124 --> 00:01:08.500
CoCurve expands that drift into a single curvature matrix, read two ways.

9
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:17.488
The diagonal is the classical importance score; the off-diagonal entries are new — co-pruning edges, the extra damage from cutting two parts together.

10
00:01:17.908 --> 00:01:29.716
Measuring every pair sounds impossible — but the whole edge matrix is a Gram product of single-part tests: probe each part once, with no pairwise sweeps, gradients, or labels.

11
00:01:29.896 --> 00:01:37.504
That turns a quadratic, million-pair measurement into a linear one — the cheapest experiment that still sees every interaction.

12
00:01:37.924 --> 00:01:48.508
Then CoCurve prunes in one shot — a greedy solver under one shared budget across attention and feed-forward, with one calibration-chosen dial for the edges.

13
00:01:48.688 --> 00:01:57.088
And it's conditional: each time a unit is removed, the remaining scores update — so CoCurve never keeps two units that were only propping each other up.

14
00:01:57.508 --> 00:02:09.340
Does it work? Across six language models and three vision-language models, CoCurve ranks first in fifty-three of sixty corpus comparisons — fifteen of fifteen at seventy billion.

15
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:16.168
On an eight-billion model at twenty percent it leads every capability group and the twelve-task average.

16
00:02:16.588 --> 00:02:27.004
And it moves the deployment frontier: higher quality at every ratio, with prefill up to one-point-five-eight times faster and peak memory down to fifty-nine percent of dense.

17
00:02:27.424 --> 00:02:39.376
The lead survives fine-tuning: after the same lightweight recovery, CoCurve's structures reach the highest quality across models — at fifty percent the eight-billion checkpoint regains ten-point-eight average points.

18
00:02:39.796 --> 00:02:50.284
And the same edges transfer to vision-language models: across three of them, CoCurve leads every average block, by one-point-one to ten-point-seven points at thirty percent.

19
00:02:50.704 --> 00:03:00.856
And it's the edges doing the work: keep only the diagonal and quality drops; add the cross-module edges and it recovers, lifting the twelve-task average by two points.

20
00:03:01.276 --> 00:03:14.692
CoCurve even predicts when edges help, from feed-forward redundancy read off the calibration matrix, and damps to a classical score where they won't. A lightweight recovery extends the lead through fifty percent.

21
00:03:15.112 --> 00:03:22.120
The takeaway is a change of unit: importance isn't in a part alone — it lives in the connections between parts.

22
00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:30.796
CoCurve reads both from one matrix, recovers the edges cheaply, and prunes them together. Prune the edges — not just the nodes.

